Oh. That one's easy; you don't.

At least not in the usual (PC) sense of dual-booting, where both OS's share
at least a common boot-loader or even a disk.

You can install and boot both VMS and AlphaNT on the same machine, provided
that the Alpha has both SRM and ARC/AlphaBios consoles, and separate disks
for VMS and NT.

1) VMS must be booted from SRM, NT must be booted from ARC/AlphaBios.
2) VMS don't understand partitions, so VMS must have it's own disk(s).
Likewise, NT doesn't know heads or tails from any of the filesystems known
to VMS.

If you have an Alpha matching these requirements, you just proceed
installing the separate OS's. Only caveat is; install NT before VMS. NT has
been known to refuse to install when an "alien" disk is present.

Since VMS and NT must be booted from separate firmware consoles, you will
have to make an extra powercycle sequence when you switch consoles.

/Lennart

